WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.073 --> 00:00:10.210
What if, someday, we decided that the
usefulness of our planet had run its course,

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:14.180
and humans needed to scour space
to find somewhere new to live?

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:19.352
But instead of finding a potentially habitable
Earth-like exoplanet light years away,

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.254
we'd build our own artificial world

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:23.890
right in the Solar System.

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:37.670
This is WHAT IF,

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:38.872
and here's what would happen

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:40.874
if we built an artificial planet.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:45.678
Rocky planets like Earth are
born from the leftover material

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.414
of a newly formed star.

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:51.885
They start off as grains of dust smaller
than the width of a human hair.

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:56.189
These grains fuse into larger chunks
that keep colliding with each other

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:58.625
until, after a few million years,

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:01.027
they develop into a new terrestrial world.

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:06.132
Theoretically, we understand how
planets are formed in the Universe.

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:09.636
But how would we go about
manufacturing an artificial one?

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:14.808
If we humans were to build a
planetary replica and populate it,

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:18.144
we'd have to churn out a rock with
an atmosphere of breathable air,

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.446
the right temperature,

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:20.947
Earth-like gravity,

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.916
and a stable orbit around the Sun.

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:24.584
And that's just for starters.

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:29.022
We'd start the construction
in the Sun's habitable zone.

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:32.892
That would help us maintain Earth-like
temperatures on our artificial planet.

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:36.463
But where would we find all
the materials to build it?

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:39.367
Asteroids might seem a good source.

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:44.370
The problem is, the Earth has the
mass of over 2,000 asteroid belts.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.973
There's simply not enough
asteroids in the Solar System

00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:49.503
to build a new, Earth-sized world.

00:01:49.503 --> 00:01:52.049
But there could be enough
materials in the Oort cloud -

00:01:52.049 --> 00:01:54.833
the swarm of icy debris that, hypothetically, 

00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:58.111
exists in the outermost
reaches of the Solar System.

00:01:58.111 --> 00:02:01.445
But this cloud is so far
away, that even Voyager 1,

00:02:01.445 --> 00:02:06.365
that's been traveling at 17 km/s
(11 mps) for the past 41 years,

00:02:06.365 --> 00:02:09.487
won't reach it for another 300 years.

00:02:09.487 --> 00:02:11.965
And it won't make it to the
other side of the Oort cloud

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:14.196
for about 30,000 years.

00:02:14.196 --> 00:02:16.198
We'd need to build some faster spacecraft

00:02:16.198 --> 00:02:19.563
to get all that dust and nudge
it in the right direction.

00:02:19.563 --> 00:02:22.802
We could also start stealing
moons from other planets.

00:02:22.802 --> 00:02:24.633
All of Jupiter's moons, for example,

00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:29.516
would give us enough material to craft
a planet about 7% the size of Earth.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:30.842
That would be a good start

00:02:30.842 --> 00:02:33.248
if we could manage to pull
them out of Jupiter's orbit.

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:38.039
It wouldn't be necessary to build
our new world as big as the Earth

00:02:38.039 --> 00:02:41.114
to replicate the same force
of gravity we feel now.

00:02:41.114 --> 00:02:43.621
If we could pack one
tenth of the Earth's mass

00:02:43.621 --> 00:02:45.527
into a sphere the size of the Moon,

00:02:45.527 --> 00:02:47.160
it would do the same trick.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:51.569
Still, it would take us at least several
hundred years to finish the construction.

00:02:53.011 --> 00:02:55.023
Once all the engineering issues were solved,

00:02:55.023 --> 00:02:58.841
and our newly formed artificial planet
settled into orbit around the Sun,

00:02:58.841 --> 00:03:00.548
we'd dump in some water.

00:03:00.548 --> 00:03:03.379
We'd let it evaporate to
create the atmosphere.

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:05.650
We could even inject some
of the carbon dioxide

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.371
released by polluting industries on Earth.

00:03:08.371 --> 00:03:10.499
Then we'd bring in the plants.

00:03:10.499 --> 00:03:13.157
Plants would slowly build up
oxygen in the atmosphere

00:03:13.157 --> 00:03:15.127
through photosynthesis.

00:03:15.127 --> 00:03:17.959
Finally, after millennia of planet-building,

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:20.229
the first human colonies would set foot

00:03:20.229 --> 00:03:23.032
on the new terrestrial
human-made planet.

00:03:23.032 --> 00:03:25.442
Even though it would be smaller in size,

00:03:25.442 --> 00:03:28.824
the Earth's replica wouldn't seem
much different from the original.

00:03:28.824 --> 00:03:33.116
Earth-like temperatures and gravity
would make it feel like our home planet. 

00:03:33.116 --> 00:03:35.460
But it wouldn't be as stable as the Earth.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:39.015
Our artificial world would
require active maintenance -

00:03:39.015 --> 00:03:40.799
from the planetary environment

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:43.279
to our orbital parameters.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.376
Currently, there's so much we
don't know about the Universe.

00:03:46.376 --> 00:03:49.561
And we can only guess how a new
planet would form within it.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:53.085
At this point, it may be easier
to terraform an existing world

00:03:53.085 --> 00:03:55.276
instead of building a totally new one.

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:59.169
But that's a story for another WHAT IF.

